#!/usr/bin/python3
# Managed by curiosity.workstation (tasks/pia-tunnel.yml).
"""Keep a fail-closed network namespace connected to Private Internet Access.

The namespace (default `pia`) holds only loopback and one WireGuard interface
to a PIA server, with nftables dropping everything else inside it. Apps started
inside it by `curiosity-pia-run` therefore have exactly two outcomes: their
traffic leaves through the tunnel, or it does not leave at all. There is no
route, no resolver and no IPv6 path around the tunnel.

Registration follows PIA's published WireGuard flow (pia-foss/manual-connections):
a token from the account login, the v6 server list, then `addKey` on port 1337
verified against PIA's own CA. The WireGuard UDP socket is created in the host
namespace and the interface is then moved into the PIA namespace, so the
encrypted packets leave through the normal host link while the plaintext side
only exists inside the namespace.

Port forwarding (optional, `port_forward` in the configuration): PIA's
getSignature/bindPort API is only reachable through the tunnel, so the manager
calls it from inside the namespace. The port belongs to one server, so the tunnel
then only connects to port-forwarding regions and keeps the server it had. The
signed grant is kept (root-only) so a rebuilt tunnel to the same server gets the
same port back; `bind` renews it every few minutes and publishes the port in
/run/curiosity-pia-tunnel/forwarded-port for the launcher's port sync.

Secrets: the account login is read from a root-only file; the login, token and
private key travel to curl and wg through stdin, never argv, and are never
logged or written anywhere (the private key lives only in memory and in the
kernel interface).

Commands (all need root):
  up               register with PIA and (re)build the tunnel
  down [--destroy] remove the tunnel; the namespace stays closed unless --destroy
  ensure           `up` when the tunnel is missing, stale or too old, else no-op
  needs-renewal    exit 0 when `ensure` would rebuild the tunnel, 1 when healthy
  bind             (re)acquire and re-bind the forwarded port; run by a timer
  status           print the tunnel state; exit 0 only when connected
"""

